We have mismatched hash calculations. In ledger.appendEntry we compute hash using computeHash(entryWithoutHash) where entryWithoutHash includes fields seq, at, kind, recordId, data, prevHash. That's okay. But test expects canonical JSON with keys sorted alphabetically: they sort Object.keys(entry).sort; our computeHash does same. So why mismatch? Possibly order of fields: we include data object with its fields unsorted; but canonical sorts top-level only, not nested data. The test builds obj = { seq, at, kind, recordId, data, prevHash } and then JSON.stringify(obj, Object.keys(obj).sort()). That sorts top-level keys, but data object's fields retain original order. In our ledger entry, data may have fields in given order; when we compute canonicalJson we do same: keys sorted = seq, at, data, kind, prevHash, recordId? Actually Object.keys(entry).sort sorts alphabetically: ['at','data','kind','prevHash','recordId','seq']. Our entryWithoutHash order doesn't matter because we sort. So should match.

But mismatch could be due to at timestamp: they use new Date().toISOString() at runtime; test expects same timestamp for both entries? In hash linking test they create two entries sequentially; first entry's at is timestamp for creation; second entry's at is later timestamp. They compute expected hash using second entry's at from ledger (the actual). In test they do:

